          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "**What the pattern means:** Your results show a raised TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone) with a free T4 in the normal range. The guidance I found describes this combination as \"subclinical hypothyroidism,\" which is distinct from overt hypothyroidism, where free T4 is also low. The guidance explains that TSH responds to small changes in thyroid hormone before the hormone levels leave the reference range, and that thyroid peroxidase antibodies can show whether an autoimmune thyroid condition is the cause and help estimate how likely the condition is to progress. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*.)\n\n**Treatment:** The retrieved material does not say what guidance recommends for treating subclinical hypothyroidism. A general reference in the same collection says that whether to treat subclinical disease is an unresolved question, and it refers to the hypothyroidism guideline for detail. That detail was not in the passages I could retrieve, so I can't tell you what guidance advises here.\n\n**Recheck interval:** I also could not find a recheck interval for subclinical results. The six-to-eight-week figure in the general hypothyroidism reference applies to people on replacement treatment after a dose change. It should not be assumed to apply to your situation.\n\n**What to do next:** Your clinician is the right person to decide whether treatment is needed and when to retest. Useful questions include whether antibody tests have been done, what your TSH level means for you specifically, and how often they would like to recheck it. Seek prompt care if you develop new symptoms, though the material I found does not list specific ones for this pattern.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
